WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.000
I have got four belly blasting supplements
you can add to your diet-

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.240
-many benefits of magnesium and how to get
more magnesium rich food-

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.300
-If you've been following my newsletter, you've
heard me talk about probiotic-

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.020
Supplements like vitamins, herbs and protein
powders comprise 36 billion dollars

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.280
in annual US retail sales.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.220
And it's almost completely unregulated.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.640
We have developed the ultimate male vitality
supplement with eight concentrated super herbs.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.940
An open marketplace has facilitated experimentation,

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.380
but it's also allowed products to flourish
like Herbalife,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.660
which some claim is a pyramid scheme that
doesn't help users lose weight.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.020
Allegations that the company denies.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.460
Now under investigation by the FBI and the
Federal Trade Commission.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:50.940
Neal Thanedar set out to bring accountability
to the market without quashing it's dynamism.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.320
You don't know what you're taking.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.920
There could be a fish oil capsule that's 10%
omega 3s, there could be one that's 90% omega 3s.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.200
There could be what we found

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.819
which is thatabout a third of the capsules that are actually on the shelf are rancid.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.880
I think what Labdoor does provides kind of
this nice balance between

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.440
some sort of regulatory function

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.940
but at the same time, it's a free market,
right?

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.540
We're, you can, like anyone can sell a product,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.120
if you do something wrong we'll catch you,
or like we'll do our best to catch you.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.100
But we're not blocking the products from the
market.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.340
We're not setting up a ten year process to
get to market.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.740
Six years ago he co-founded Labdoor

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.040
with the goal of bringing more transparency
to the supplements industry.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.820
The company has received funding from several
prominent angel investors and venture capitalists

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.660
including Mark Cuban and Y Combinator.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.160
Are there any results that have really surprised
you

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:46.330
either in terms of 'wow this has exactly what
it says and is pretty effective'

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.710
or falling way short of what it's promise
is?

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.890
Yeah so there's certain categories, if you
look at like a vitamin D or magnesium

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.280
or kind of these simple ingredients, like
creatine,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.880
you'll find that the vast majority of products
will be very good, you know,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.940
so it might be 70, 80, 90% of the market is
clean.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.100
And then there's other categories like Garcinia
Cambogia

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.369
is something that Doctor Oz had been really
pushing heavily

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:14.980
and when we tested in 2017 we found that 70%
of the products didn't have the active ingredients

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.320
up to the label.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.940
The company buys the products right off the
shelves

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.300
and then posts the test results for free on its website.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.220
It makes money when people click through to
purchase an item.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.100
People might think that there's a conflict
of interest there

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.340
if you are testing these products but also
in a way marketing them.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.140
How do you justify that?

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.720
Well often find is that it's not the most
popular product that wins categories

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.890
it's not the thing that the big brand names
that you might have heard of

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.980
often is like a B or a C grade on our site.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.060
The real top A grade products tend to be something
that people haven't ever heard of before.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:57.780
So it's the protein powder from England, it's
the fish oil from Belgium.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.800
These companies in some cases have never even
sold in the US

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.300
and once we find them, we start testing, they
actually find a market in the US

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.640
to actually start selling.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.520
Fish oil doesn't sound that far from snake
oil.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.820
What did you go in thinking about supplements

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.400
and have you changed the way you thought about
dietary supplements?

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.490
I think I probably went through three phases
of my life

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.660
so like when I was in high school and I was
playing sports

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.650
it was like I would take the protein powders
and the creatines and whatever

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.170
I just wanted to, I would buy whatever was
the most popular right

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.980
or whatever like my friend recommended, right,
and that's the classic thing that people start with.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.580
and then, you know, I went to college and
I started researching the science behind it

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.660
and I was just hit me and I kind of went the
other way and I was like I don't want to take anything

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.239
because I, like I don't know what's in these
products, right?

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:47.630
And then once we started testing these products,
you can definitely find, like

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:51.800
we found protein powders that say they had,
they claimed 25 grams of protein per scoop

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.780
and they had 5 or 10 grams.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.820
And someone took the worst product every single
day, they would actually absolutely get sick, right?

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.440
And if they took the worst fish oil that had
10% or less Omega 3s

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.860
it wouldn't be that effective, right?

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.620
And so this idea of we can find out what's
safe and effective just by testing the products

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.459
and if we can do that then if you only took
the best products

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.960
then you would actually see an effect.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.320
And so I think the supplement has a lot of
great players and a lot of bad players, right?

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.940
And our job is to kind of be the referee and
kind of separate the two.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:28.260
Labdoor once tested male libido supplements
marketed by Infowars founder Alex Jones.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.960
Though taking them is unlikely to increase
libido,

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.180
they probably also won't do much harm the
company found.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.740
Both sides got what they wanted out of that
story, like

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.300
Alex Jones was really excited that it was
all pure

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.720
and I think the other side of the media was
very, kind of frustrated by the, kind of,

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.060
the extreme claims that were being made
about what might actually happen with your health.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.220
For us, like, we do the testing, we tell you
what actually happened

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.350
and then you can read that very different
ways.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.660
Thanedar plans to create a decentralized network
of laboratories called TEST.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:04.540
Consumers would no longer have to trust a
single third party, including Labdoor or the FDA.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.860
Instead, through a decentralized database
known as a blockchain

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.350
participants, including the supplement manufacturers
themselves

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:18.820
could actually place bets using tokens issued
by TEST as to whether a product would pass muster.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:23.760
In a classic certification, someone, like
the company would pay for a certification

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.160
and we would, like anyone would deliver it.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.400
And I think that, that centralization can
cause kind of some bias

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.979
when you're directly paying the people who
give you a rating

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.020
with like a credit rating agency, you have
a lot of risk.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:40.120
We want to use this token as that curation
mechanism for this registry

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.920
so that we can basically have an open, decentralized
way

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.040
for one, any lab can come in and if they're
registered, if they pass the testing,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.120
they can actually, they can contribute,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.300
they can actually make money as a testing lab for this registry.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.280
And so it doesn't just have to be us, anyone
can do testing.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.860
And so I think it becomes a self selection
mechanism

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.770
where the best companies, the best products
will actually rise to the top

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.880
by just the factor of this kind of curation
this prediction system

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.760
and the idea of, kind of, the best companies
kind of self select into the market.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.320
And Thanedar says his vision of a market solution
to consumer safety concerns

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.360
isn't limited to supplements.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:24.400
Labdoor has it's sights set on another giant
market: marijuana.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.900
It's a market that has basically come out
of nowhere

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.260
and there's a lot of testing to be done, there's
a lot of inconsistencies.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:34.139
Here in California, now with recreational
legalization

00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:37.820
there's actually mandatory testing and there's
a whole system of labs

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.730
so how does your vision fit into that?

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:44.340
I think the biggest thing for that is convincing
people to test publicly.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.790
Don't, like there's a hundred things on the
shelf

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.720
you don't want to know the 10 things that
fail, you want to know the number one best thing, right?

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.900
Like, if there was like a spotlight that like,
was like this was the number one best product

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.740
and you could just take it off and walk out
the store that would be magical, right?

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.720
And the kind of experience we're trying to
make

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.060
is like take those hundred products and like
put the spotlight on the one best product.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.120
And that's, more than just saying hey it passed
government,

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.169
like the minimum government regulations,

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:09.350
it's like the best product just buy this and
you'll be happy.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.520
And that's the thing that we're really trying
to get to.

